1. Horizontal gene resistance, plasmid identification & virulence factor (ABRicate + hAMRonization or abriTAMR)
Two options: use ABRicate for AMR, plasmid identification, and virulence factor,
standardize AMR with hAMRonization and create a heatmap for virulence factor and plasmid.
OR
Use abriTAMR for acquired and point mutations, and ABRicate for plasmid & virulence factor.
Torsten Seeman's group (previously created ABRicate), is responsible for developing abriTAMR.

RUNNING abriTAMR for both acquired gene and point mutation resistance
Prepare species_samplesheet.csv using R to specify the species scheme for abriTAMR.
Compile all results in /<R project>/inputs folder
Activate conda & abricate env. first.

It is preferrable to make a new folder and re-analyse all samples.
I usually change old folder to output_abritamr_old

mkdir -p /srv/nfs_share/2026_ACORNHAI/inputs/output_abritamr; \
tail -n +2 /srv/nfs_share/2026_ACORNHAI/inputs/species_samplesheet.csv | \
while IFS=, read -r workAMR_fasta mlst abritamr_scheme; do \
	b=$(basename "$workAMR_fasta" .fastq.gz.long.fasta); \
	abritamr run --species "$abritamr_scheme" \
		--contigs "$workAMR_fasta"\
		--prefix /srv/nfs_share/2026_ACORNHAI/inputs/output_abritamr/"${b%.*}";
done

Rename abriTAMR result based on barcodes from previous folder:
for i in /srv/nfs_share/2026_ACORNHAI/inputs/output_abritamr/*/*; do
	folder_name=$(basename "$(dirname "$i")")
	b=$(basename "$i")
	mv "$i" "$(dirname "$i")/${folder_name}_${b}"
done

Save database version used in ABRicate for this project as a text file in /inputs
abricate --list > /srv/nfs_share/2026_ACORNHAI/inputs/db_abricate
mkdir -p /srv/nfs_share/2026_ACORNHAI/inputs/output_abricate; \
tail -n +2 /srv/nfs_share/2026_ACORNHAI/inputs/species_samplesheet.csv | \
while IFS=, read -r workAMR_fasta mlst abritamr_scheme; do \
	b=$(basename "$workAMR_fasta" .fastq.gz.long.fasta); \
	for db in plasmidfinder vfdb; do \
		abricate --db "$db" "$workAMR_fasta" > /srv/nfs_share/2026_ACORNHAI/inputs/output_abricate/${b}_abricate_${db}.tab; \
	done; \
done; \
for db in plasmidfinder vfdb; do \
    abricate --summary /srv/nfs_share/2026_ACORNHAI/inputs/output_abricate/*abricate_${db}.tab > /srv/nfs_share/2026_ACORNHAI/inputs/abricate_${db}_wide.tab; \
done

ADDITIONAL protocol for analysing plasmids from Acetinobacter baumannii (PlasmidFinder's df is originally designed for Enterococcus)
SOURCE: https://www.sciencedirect.com/org/science/article/pii/S2165049722006242
fasta df downloaded from: https://github.com/MehradHamidian/AcinetobacterPlasmidTyping/blob/main
I personally compile ALL of the Abaumannii's plasmid type into a FASTA file called acinetobacterplasmidtyper_compiled.fasta (update 8 July 2026)
stored in a designated folder /inputs/db_abaumannii_plasmid
consisting of:
Rep/rep database
https://github.com/MehradHamidian/AcinetobacterPlasmidTyping/blob/main/2026_May_rep_DNA-seqs_V3.fasta
Mob/mob database
https://github.com/MehradHamidian/AcinetobacterPlasmidTyping/blob/main/2026_May_mob_DNA-seqs_V1.fasta
ParA/parA database
https://github.com/MehradHamidian/AcinetobacterPlasmidTyping/blob/main/2026_May_parA_DNA-seqs_V1.fasta
CIS database (Conserved Intergenic Region)
https://github.com/MehradHamidian/AcinetobacterPlasmidTyping/blob/main/2026_May_CIS_DNA-seqs_V1.fasta

cat /srv/nfs_share/2026_ACORNHAI/inputs/db_abaumannii_plasmid/*.fasta > /srv/nfs_share/2026_ACORNHAI/inputs/acinetobacterplasmidtyper_compiled.fasta

Prepare species_samplesheet_Abaumannii_plasmid.csv using R to list all Abaumannii isolates.

mkdir -p /srv/nfs_share/2026_ACORNHAI/inputs/output_abaumannii_plasmid_blastn; \
tail -n +2 /srv/nfs_share/2026_ACORNHAI/inputs/species_samplesheet_abaumannii_plasmid.csv | \
while IFS=, read -r workAMR_fasta mlst_scheme amr; do \
	b=$(basename "$workAMR_fasta" .fastq.gz.long.fasta); \
	blastn -query /srv/nfs_share/2026_ACORNHAI/inputs/acinetobacterplasmidtyper_compiled.fasta \
		-subject "$workAMR_fasta" \
		-outfmt 6 \
		-perc_identity 95 \
		-out /srv/nfs_share/2026_ACORNHAI/inputs/output_abaumannii_plasmid_blastn/${b}.txt;
done



2. MLST typing
Prepare species_samplesheet.csv using R to specify the MLST scheme based on bacterial genus/species.

tail -n +2 /srv/nfs_share/2026_ACORNHAI/inputs/species_samplesheet.csv | \
while IFS=, read -r workAMR_fasta mlst_scheme amr; do \
	mlst --scheme "$mlst_scheme" \
		"$workAMR_fasta"; done > /srv/nfs_share/2026_ACORNHAI/inputs/mlst_all.tsv

Open new terminal and change owner to let the files be accessed with R
sudo chown -R acorn2 /srv/nfs_share/2026_ACORNHAI/inputs














4. Variant calling using minimap2, samtools & PEPPER (through docker)
conda activate align_tools

Previous guide from KEMRI training (October 2024, 2. Evaluate the pipeline command and results2.docx) used FASTQ mannually merged from fastq_pass folder, including DCS removal.
I skip this process and used fastqs generated by GHRU-mod (I modified the pipeline for including fastq copy to /fastqs folder in the output folder).

Previous KEMRI training guide (October 2024, 2. Evaluate the pipeline command and the results.2.docx) used basecalled FASTQs manually merged from the /fastq_pass folder, including DCS removal.
I skipped this step and instead used FASTQs stored in /fastqs generated by GHRU-mod (I modified the pipeline to include fastq copy to the /fastqs subdirectory in the output folder).

Because each sample MUST be variant-called with specific bacterial species reference (Ref.) genome, I designed an executable loop for each sample listed in a *csv.




minimap2 -a Kleb.pneuMGH78578.fasta mysample001_only_reads.fastq > aligned_long_reads.sam
samtools view -S -b aligned_long_reads.sam | samtools sort -o sorted_long_reads.bam
samtools index sorted_long_reads.bam
samtools rmdup sorted_long_reads.bam deduplicated.bam
samtools faidx Kleb.pneuMGH78578.fasta
mkdir reference
mv Kleb.pneuMGH78578.fasta Kleb.pneuMGH78578.fasta.fai sorted_long_reads.bam sorted_long_reads.bam.bai reference 













